# Task
# Caça-Palavras — implementation brief

You are a software engineer implementing a small web game from this specification. You have a shell in the repository root. Read this whole document once, then work strictly milestone by milestone (§12). Everything you need is here; do not invent features that are not specified, and do not skip anything that is.

## 0. How to work

1. Do only the milestone you were asked to do. When it is done, stop and report.
2. After every milestone, run `npm run check` (typecheck + unit tests + production build). It must pass before you report. If it fails: read the error, fix the cause, re-run. Never delete or weaken a test, never add `// @ts-ignore` or `any` to get past an error.
3. Allowed npm dependencies, nothing else: `vite`, `typescript`, `vitest`, `vite-plugin-pwa`, `@fontsource/nunito`. No React/Vue/Svelte, no Phaser/Pixi, no canvas, no jQuery, no CSS frameworks.
4. Code in `src/core/` is pure TypeScript with no DOM, `window`, `document` or `navigator` access, so it runs in Node tests. DOM code lives only in `src/ui/`.
5. Keep every file under 250 lines. Split modules exactly as laid out in §3.
6. All user-visible text comes from `src/ui/strings.ts`, whose content is given verbatim in Appendix C. Do not paraphrase these strings; the app is in Brazilian Portuguese.
7. Data files (`src/data/words.ts`, `src/data/letters.ts`) are given verbatim in Appendices A and B. If they already exist in the repo, do not modify them. If not, create them exactly as written.
8. Never leave `TODO` comments or placeholder implementations behind. If something is out of scope, it is listed in §1.3 — leave it out entirely.
9. Commit at the end of each milestone: `git add -A && git commit -m "M<n>: <milestone title>"`.
10. Final report format: (a) files created/changed, (b) the last 15 lines of `npm run check` output, (c) anything you could not do and why.

## 1. Product

### 1.1 What it is

A word search game (caça-palavras) for Brazilian Portuguese speakers, played on a phone in portrait orientation by dragging a finger across a letter grid. It is a Progressive Web App: installable, fully offline, no accounts, no server.

The game is cozy by design: there is no timer, no score, no penalty for wrong guesses, and hints are unlimited. The only progression is completing puzzles inside themed word lists (fruits, the beach, festa junina...). The visual metaphor is a highlighter pen on paper: found words are highlighter-colored pills drawn behind the letters.

### 1.2 Core loop

1. Home screen lists themes with progress ("4 de 12").
2. Tapping a theme opens its next unsolved puzzle (12 puzzles per theme, 4 difficulty tiers × 3 puzzles).
3. The puzzle screen shows the letter grid and the list of words to find, as chips.
4. The player presses a letter and drags; the selection snaps to one of 8 directions and is drawn as a translucent yellow pill. Releasing on a listed word (read forwards or backwards) locks the pill in a color, strikes the chip, and gives a short vibration. Releasing on anything else just fades the pill out.
5. Tapping a chip pulses the first letter of that word in the grid (a hint). Unlimited.
6. When all listed words are found, a completion card offers "Próxima fase" or "Escolher tema".
7. Progress (solved puzzles, words found in the current puzzle, settings) persists in `localStorage`.

### 1.3 Explicitly out of scope

Timers, scores, streaks, leaderboards, ads, accounts, sound effects, landscape layouts, multi-language UI, a level editor, server calls of any kind, and analytics. Do not build any of these.

## 2. Stack and constraints

- Vite + TypeScript (strict), vanilla DOM, ES modules. One HTML file, hash routing.
- Unit tests with Vitest, Node environment only (no jsdom).
- PWA via `vite-plugin-pwa` (Workbox precache of the built app shell, `autoUpdate`).
- Font: Nunito (rounded, friendly, uppercase I/J/L and O/Q clearly distinct) via `@fontsource/nunito`, bundled so it works offline. Fallback `system-ui, sans-serif`.
- Target: Android Chrome first, then any modern mobile browser. Layout column max width 480px, centered; works from 320px wide.
- The grid is rendered with CSS grid for letters and an SVG overlay for pills. No canvas.
- Rendering must never depend on network availability.

## 3. Repository layout

```
package.json
tsconfig.json
vite.config.ts
vitest.config.ts
index.html
public/icons/icon.svg
src/main.ts                 # boot: load save, register router, first render
src/style.css               # tokens + all styles (single file, ≤ 250 lines)
src/core/types.ts           # shared types (§5)
src/core/normalize.ts       # normalizeWord, isValidWord
src/core/prng.ts            # hashSeed, mulberry32, rngInt, shuffle
src/core/directions.ts      # Dir, DIRS, DIR_ORDER, isDiagonal, angleOf, maxSteps, pathCells
src/core/levels.ts          # TIERS, tierForIndex, PUZZLES_PER_THEME, puzzleKey
src/core/generate.ts        # generatePuzzle, findOccurrences, GenerationError
src/core/select.ts          # snapSelection (pointer → direction + steps)
src/core/save.ts            # SaveState shape, default, migrate, (de)serialize helpers (pure)
src/data/words.ts           # THEMES (Appendix A)
src/data/letters.ts         # LETTER_WEIGHTS (Appendix B)
src/data/blocklist.ts       # BLOCKLIST: string[] (see §6.6)
src/ui/strings.ts           # STR (Appendix C)
src/ui/router.ts            # parseHash, navigate, onRoute
src/ui/storage.ts           # localStorage read/write wrapping core/save
src/ui/home.ts              # home screen render
src/ui/puzzle.ts            # puzzle screen render + state
src/ui/grid.ts              # grid DOM + SVG overlay + sizing
src/ui/pointer.ts           # pointer events → core/select → grid pills
src/ui/settings.ts          # settings sheet
src/core/*.test.ts          # tests next to the module they test
```

